   ],
   "source": [
    "sampled = np.array(list(tide_heights.values()))\n",
    "\n",
    "metrics = pit.coverage.coverage_metrics(sampled, series_heights)\n",
    "verdict = pit.coverage.coverage_verdict(metrics)\n",
    "pit.coverage.print_coverage_report(metrics, verdict,\n",
    "                                   title=f\"Tidal sampling · {aoi.name}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "3b3a63d2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Three views of the tidal frame over the whole period:\n",
    "#   left    every modelled tide height — the envelope the estuary experiences\n",
    "#   centre  only the heights the satellite caught\n",
    "#   right   those same heights against the true extremes, so the band no\n",
    "#           image reaches is a distance you can read in metres\n",
    "sampled_times = pd.to_datetime(sorted(tide_heights))\n",
    "sampled = np.array([tide_heights[d] for d in sorted(tide_heights)])\n",
    "\n",
    "viz.plot_tide_record(series_times, series_heights, sampled_times, sampled,\n",
    "                     model_name=TIDE_MODEL, location=tide_point);"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3d008ac0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 8 · Elevation\n",
    "\n",
    "Our estimator, **HSR**, uses the physics of mixed pixels. A 10 m pixel on the\n",
    "waterline is *partially* flooded, so its NDWI is the mixture of its wet and dry\n",
    "parts, and as the tide rises the wet fraction traces the pixel's internal\n",
    "hypsometry. Fitting that sigmoid per pixel gives three things at once:\n",
    "\n",
    "* **μ** — the elevation (the whole curve is used, so no threshold crossing);\n",
    "* **σ** — the relief *inside* the pixel, a sub-pixel product;\n",
    "* **σ(μ)** — a Cramér–Rao uncertainty, offered as quality control.\n",
    "\n",
    "Fitted per **epoch**, not per decade: intertidal morphology migrates, and\n",
    "fitting ten years at once blurs the transition. Measured here on the same\n",
    "pixels with only the time window changed, σ saturates the top of the fitting\n",
    "grid for 12 % of pixels over ten years against 6 % over three, and the fit\n",
    "residual drops from 0.187 to 0.171 NDWI.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Read the uncertainty with caution.** A split-half test (the dates halved\n",
    "> into two sets with matched tidal coverage, fitted independently) puts the\n",
    "> typical pixel's repeatability at ~5 cm, but the Cramér–Rao value predicts\n",
    "> ~3 cm where ~10 cm is observed, and does not separate stable pixels from\n",
    "> unstable ones within the bulk of the distribution. It is not yet calibrated;\n",
    "> treat it as indicative. See section 9."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "30fe0e1f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "  epoch 2016-2016: 16 dates\n",
      "  epoch 2017-2019: 101 dates\n",
      "  epoch 2020-2022: 91 dates\n",
      "  epoch 2023-2025: 97 dates\n",
      "\n",
      "fitting the most recent epoch: 2023-2025\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# ── Parameters ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n",
    "EPOCH_YEARS     = 3       # morphology is stable enough within ~3 years\n",
    "SCALE           = 4       # super-resolution factor: 10 m → 2.5 m\n",
    "MAX_UNCERTAINTY = 0.10    # drop pixels whose σ(μ) exceeds 10 cm\n",
    "SIGMA_FLOOR     = 0.05    # noise floor of σ, not injected as relief\n",
    "\n",
    "epoch_list = pit.epochs(sorted(tide_heights), epoch_years=EPOCH_YEARS)\n",
    "for e in epoch_list:\n",
    "    print(f\"  epoch {e['label']}: {len(e['dates'])} dates\")\n",
    "\n",
    "latest = epoch_list[-1]\n",
    "print(f\"\\nfitting the most recent epoch: {latest['label']}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# ── HSR fit (local: no cloud job) ───────────────────────────────────────\n",
    "dem = pit.fit_hsr(\n",
    "    cube, tide_heights,\n",
    "    dates=latest[\"dates\"],\n",
    "    scale=SCALE,\n",
    "    sigma_floor=SIGMA_FLOOR,\n",
    "    max_uncertainty=MAX_UNCERTAINTY,\n",
    "    clip_mask=intertidal,\n",
    "    epoch_label=latest[\"label\"],\n",
    ")\n",
    "dem.summary()\n",
    "\n",
    "log.record(\"HSR elevation\", kind=\"apply\",\n",
    "           params={\"epoch\": latest[\"label\"], \"scale\": SCALE,\n",
    "                   \"max_uncertainty\": MAX_UNCERTAINTY},\n",
    "           inputs=[f\"{len(latest['dates'])} dates\", \"tide heights\"],\n",
    "           outputs=[\"elevation\", \"sub-pixel relief\", \"uncertainty\"])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "fb2ca20b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1650x1500 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "viz.plot_dem(dem.mu, transform, crs, aoi,\n",
    "             title=f\"Intertidal elevation · {aoi.name} · epoch {dem.epoch}\");"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "e9ccd414",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1900x800 with 4 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(19, 8))\n",
    "viz.plot_uncertainty(dem.sigma_mu, transform, crs, aoi,\n",
    "                     title=\"Elevation uncertainty (Cramér–Rao)\", ax=axes[0])\n",
    "viz.plot_subpixel_relief(dem.sigma, transform, crs, aoi,\n",
    "                         title=\"Sub-pixel relief σ\", ax=axes[1])\n",
    "fig.tight_layout();"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2fd990ce",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### The published baseline, for comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "The DEA-style **step** method reads the tide at which the rolling median of\n",
    "NDWI crosses from dry to wet. It is more selective than HSR — it needs a clean\n",
    "monotonic transition — and provides no uncertainty, but it is the reference\n",
    "method of the literature and runs from the same cube."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "0a6b8498",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stderr",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
